fact WorkItem(id: "WI-205", description: some(value: "Modify framework runtime: per-resource handlers + Cell sort (proposal 037 §2-§3 foundation). Builds the mechanism behind Modify before downstream consumers (WI-203 WorkItemStore, future domain stores) can land cleanly. Three pieces: (a) per-resource Modify handler dispatch — refactor eval/effects.rs default_modify_handler into a dispatcher that looks up per-resource-functor handlers in HashMap (Symbol, Box (dyn ModifyResourceHandler)). Each handler owns its state representation. The dispatcher routes Modify.get/set on a value v to the handler registered for v functor; absence of a handler is an error per Rule 8 (Modify[T] requires a registered handler). Default behavior for bare entities goes away — types must be explicitly Modifiable. (b) Cell sort + Cell-specific handler — add stdlib/anthill/prelude/cell.anthill declaring sort Cell with sort V = ? plus operations new(initial: V) -> Cell[V], get(c: Cell[V]) -> V, set(c: Cell[V], value: V) -> Unit effects Modify[c]. Register a Cell handler at startup that owns a Cell-specific arena (HashMap-backed in v0.1; future allocation-time uid via WI-200). Cell is the canonical small-state Modifiable sort all consumers can use. (c) Modifiable[T] marker — formalize Rule 8 typing. Either as a fact (fact Modifiable[T = Cell[V]]) populated when a sort registers a handler, or via a kernel-side registration check. Typer rejects Modify[T] in effect rows when T has no Modifiable fact. (Strict enforcement may follow as a separate WI; v0.1 may relax to compile-time-warn.) Migration: existing bundle host (run_anthill_bundle) currently relies on default_modify_handler accepting any entity; update to register specific handlers per the resources it uses (the WIS handler for the bundle). Tests: per-resource isolation (two distinct sorts with separate handlers do not share state); Cell.new returns a fresh handle (modulo WI-200 multi-instance); Modify on a non-Modifiable type errors at runtime (typing-level enforcement is a follow-up). Acceptance: cargo test green; the existing single default_modify_handler is replaced by per-resource dispatch; Cell sort loads and Cell.new/get/set are reachable from anthill source. Driver: WI-203 (WorkItemStore spec + impl) needs this mechanism in place to build correctly per proposal 037 §3 architecture (current WI-203 description assumed the v0.1 transitional functor-keyed scheme would suffice; per design feedback it does not — we should build on the right fundamentals)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-202"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-05-13T13:33:41Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-205", author: "user", content: "Lifecycle concern: under v0.1 functor-only identity, Cell.new is broken for deep recursion. Each Cell.new returns the same Value::Entity { functor: Cell, named: [] }; all calls write to the same Modify slot keyed by Cell symbol; each overwrites the previous. Recursive code with multiple distinct cells aliases. Beyond just leaky — semantically broken. Proper fix is the arena pattern already used by Map/Substitution/Stream: a cell_arena on the Interpreter, Value::Cell(handle) variant, refcount tracking, slot reclamation when refcount hits zero (overlaps WI-200 opaque-handle identity). v0.1 of WI-205 documents this limitation; honest impl deferred. Either: (a) expand WI-205 to include the cell_arena, intertwining with WI-200 (multi-instance); (b) keep WI-205 minimal and file the cell_arena as its own follow-up WI separate from WI-200 (since the arena is needed even if the identity scheme stays functor-only — recursion still needs lifecycle). Recommend (b) — file a tight follow-up WI for cell_arena + refcount lifecycle.", at: "2026-05-08T10:56:50Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-205", author: "user", content: "Delivered minimum scope (a) + (b): runtime per-resource dispatch and Cell sort are in place. Cell sort + arena landed earlier (cell_arena.rs with allocation-time uid, refcount lifecycle, no aliasing). This commit extends default_modify_handler in eval/effects.rs to dispatch on Value variant: a Value::Cell(h) arm routes Modify.get/set through interp.read_cell/write_cell (the cell arena); Value::Entity / Value::Term retain the functor-keyed in-memory map fallback for anonymous resources (m5_modify tests still pass). Realizes WI-205 (a) at the Value-variant level rather than as a Symbol→handler registry — simpler and matches the cell-runtime.md architecture where 'the per-resource handler mechanism is realized as the Cell builtin owning its arena.' (c) Modifiable marker: sort + facts already exist (Cell, FileStore, IndexedFileStore, KB). Typer-side enforcement (Modify[T] requires Modifiable[T] fact) is explicitly deferred per WI text ('v0.1 may relax to compile-time-warn'); the strict-enforcement follow-up is WI-207 (acyclic_cell typer rule) plus WI-206 (is_modifiable reflect op). Test added in wi205_cell_test.rs (modify_set_get_on_cell_routes_through_arena) confirms distinct cells stay independent under Modify.set/get — the v0.1 functor-only-aliasing bug from feedback above does not reappear. Stale comment in stdlib/anthill/prelude/cell.anthill updated to reflect the actual arena-slot identity scheme. Cargo test (full anthill-core + anthill-todo + anthill-stl + dispatch + cell): all green. Suite-perf side-trip: identified macOS Gatekeeper/XProtect scanning unsigned test binaries as the dominant cost (~4–17s per launch); cargo-nextest run cuts the full anthill-core suite from 17 min → 31 s by overlapping binary launches. Eval test files merged (6→1) for additional rebuild savings.", at: "2026-05-13T13:33:37Z")
